  <!-- ============ CHAPTER III: JOBSWINONA ============ -->
  <section class="chapter" id="jobswinona">
    <div class="chapter-label">Chapter III</div>
    <h2>The One That Should've Worked</h2>
    <p>This one's worth a little extra context, because of where the idea actually came from.</p>
    <p>I was running Facebook ads for a temp agency at the time — one of the clients you saw a couple chapters back. The idea for a local job listing site came out of that work. I pitched it to them; they passed on it. So instead of shelving it, I just built it myself.</p>
    <p>That's the part I actually want you to notice, more than the failure itself: following your curiosity and doing the work, even on a side idea nobody else wanted, can hand you the next opportunity. This particular one didn't pan out — but the muse pointed somewhere real.</p>
    <p>In 2015 I built that local job listing site — a simple place connecting job seekers with openings around town. On paper, the demand was real:</p>
    <ul class="case-list">
      <li>"Jobs in Winona MN" was pulling 1,900+ searches a month</li>
      <li>The local newspaper charged $100+ per listing and ran 20+ listings a week</li>
      <li>One employer alone was spending $100K a year on job ads</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The site made some money — display ads, affiliate clicks through Indeed, even a consulting contract. But nowhere close to what the numbers above said it could.</p>
    <div class="callout">Here's the mistake, plain and simple: I relied entirely on the website to sell itself. I never picked up the phone and called the businesses who were already paying for job ads elsewhere.</div>
    <p>Eventually I gave up and sold it on Flippa for $500. I regret that more than the failure itself — I sold the asset instead of fixing the actual problem.</p>
    <p>I wrote up the whole story, mistakes and all — worth a read if you've ever built something with real demand behind it and still watched it stall:</p>
    <p><a href="https://redefiningretirement.io/p/one-big-reason-small-online-businesses-fail">"One Big Reason Small Online Businesses Fail" →</a></p>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ CHAPTER IV: THROUGH-LINE ============ -->
  <section class="chapter" id="throughline">
    <div class="chapter-label">Chapter IV</div>
    <h2>What It All Has in Common</h2>
    <p>Here's what ties this whole list together: none of it came with a boss telling me what to do next, and none of it came with a plan I followed step by step.</p>
    <p>Some of these made real money for years. Some made about $10 every few months. One made me $500 and a lesson I still think about. A couple I just didn't stick with long enough to find out what they could've become.</p>
    <p>I'm still adding to this list every time I remember something else I tried. That's kind of the point — there wasn't a five-step system underneath any of it. There was just a willingness to try the next thing, and to keep the ones that worked.</p>
    <div class="pull">There's no single right door. There are a lot of them, and most of them aren't locked.</div>
    <p>If you're staring at your own version of this — some skill, some hobby, some weird idea you haven't taken seriously — this list is your reminder of that.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ CASSETTE DIVIDER — slot 3 ============ -->
  <div class="cassette-divider"><img src="/images/cassette-boombox.jpg" alt=""></div>

  <!-- ============ BACK MATTER ============ -->
  <section class="backmatter" id="next">
    <div class="chapter-label">What's Next</div>
    <h2 style="font-size:30px; margin-bottom:22px;">Would You Like to Try Any of These Yourself?</h2>
    <p>If any of these sparked your interest — affiliate marketing, a podcast, a digital product, consulting, whatever — tell me which one and I'll point you in the right direction.</p>
    <p>Even if I don't remember every detail, I can usually help you find someone who does and save you some time.</p>

    <div class="callout" style="margin-top:0;">
      <strong>Email me directly:</strong> tell me which one you want to try, and I'll reply with what I actually know.<br>
      <a href="mailto:coreysretirement@fastmail.com?subject=I%20want%20to%20try%20one%20of%20these&amp;body=Hey%20Corey%2C%20I%27d%20like%20to%20try%3A%20" style="font-weight:700;">coreysretirement@fastmail.com →</a>
    </div>

    <p class="script" style="font-size:22px; color:var(--gej-accent);">— Corey</p>

